  references:
    version: 2
    RefIds:
    - rid: 7221127559446265912
      type: {class: StringSettingValue, ns: , asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: Welcome to the load system tool. A tool which will allow you to save
          data without using PlayerPrefs. It's flexible and can store a lot of serialized
          types.
    - rid: 7221127563055202309
      type: {class: StringSettingValue, ns: , asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: Group values has a lot of diferent uses and cases. Most of all is
          an scriptable object in which you can store almost any type of value. In
          scripts you can set and get diferent values that you can store later as
          persistent data.
    - rid: 7221127563055202310
      type: {class: StringSettingValue, ns: , asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: Basically every type. The tool can store in a defined generic structured
          all primitives(int, bool, char, byte,etc)
    - rid: 7221127563055202311
      type: {class: StringSettingValue, ns: , asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: 'When you want to get a certain type of value you will ask for the
          name of value that has the same type. So for example, if you want an int
          value with name "speed" to your variable topSpeed the correct way to write
          is:

          GroupValues myValues;

          int topSpeed=myValues.GetValue<int>("speed");

'
    - rid: 7221127563055202313
      type: {class: StringSettingValue, ns: , asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: "For everything! Your game data can be stored in group values. Your
          settings data can be stored too. It allows you to store all your game related
          data. \nIn fact this documentation is stored in GroupValues!\nHave fun!"
    - rid: 7221127563055202315
      type: {class: StringSettingValue, ns: , asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: Templates are your Group Values readonly data. When building your
          game the preprocessor will set your data to default values. But what if
          you want certain entries with different defult values? Templates provide
          a solution to fix a default value to a Group Values reference.
    - rid: 7221127563055202317
      type: {class: StringSettingValue, ns: , asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: Group Values is just the tip of the iceberg. All data stored in them
          is not persistent :( . However loaders are here to solve this problem.
    - rid: 7221127563055202319
      type: {class: StringSettingValue, ns: , asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: Templates need a Group Values reference. Once referenced it can access
          to the entry values and set a default value for them. You do not need further
          set up. The preprocessor will automatically set those values as default
          to the referenced Group Values.
    - rid: 7221127563055202321
      type: {class: StringSettingValue, ns: , asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: 'This is a C# pure class. It takes a Group Value reference and can
          convert it to a json file. You can set your GroupValues name and a path
          to start saving your data. However you can not use it as a component. '
    - rid: 7221127563055202323
      type: {class: StringSettingValue, ns: , asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: 'In the same way you can get a value out of the class you can store
          it as well in the scriptable object. Following the same example if you
          want it to asign a value and then store it the correct way to do it is:

          GroupValues
          myValues;

          int topSpeed=2;

          myValues.SetSpeed<int>("speed",topSpeed);'
    - rid: 7221127563055202325
      type: {class: StringSettingValue, ns: , asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: If the key does not exist it will throw an error. This is important
          to know because it can crash the game. It will also throw an error if an
          invalid cast is provided.
    - rid: 7221127563055202327
      type: {class: StringSettingValue, ns: , asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: It is very important for the user to know that this methods store
          the values inside the SO(Scriptable Object) but it does not mean persistance
          in your data stored. For more information read Loaders
    - rid: 7221127563055202329
      type: {class: StringSettingValue, ns: , asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: If the key does not exist it will throw an error. This is important
          to know because it can crash the game. It will also throw an error if an
          invalid cast is provided.
    - rid: 7221127563055202331
      type: {class: StringSettingValue, ns: , asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: Have you ever tried to move a file .asset to a different project?
          Even though the SO script is in the project unity will remove all data.
          This tool comes with a solution. You can export your GroupValues.asset
          to a json or xml file to then import such file in other projects. This
          means you can recover Group Values and reuse them for future projects!
          Yay!
    - rid: 7221127563055202333
      type: {class: StringSettingValue, ns: , asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: By selecting Tools->LoadSystem->Export Group Values you will open
          the import window. Select your file with the same file extension. Then
          select somewhere inside Assets to save the new Group Values (it is recomended
          to save it within a Resources folder).
    - rid: 7221127563055202335
      type: {class: StringSettingValue, ns: , asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: If you know the name of the field in which the value is stored you
          can write GetValue<T>(fieldName,key) and SetValue<T>(fieldName,key,value).
          It is way faster.
    - rid: 7221127563055202337
      type: {class: StringSettingValue, ns: , asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: 'Although GroupValues is a flexible SO in which you can store anything
          it comes structured. The smallest element is called entry, in which you
          can store a value. All entries are defined within a field. All fields are
          confined in the GroupValue. That way you can store values in a certain
          area inside the SO.


          GroupValues

          -Field

          ---Entry


          Example:


          Settings

          -SoundSettings

          --Mute(bool)

          --MasterVolume(float)'
    - rid: 7221127563055202339
      type: {class: StringSettingValue, ns: , asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: Inside a Template you should not change anything other than values.
          This means that changing a field name or entry name can lead errors or
          to unexpected behaviour in worst cases. The window editor will not let
          you commit these actions. For more information, read Group Values Editor
    - rid: 7221127563055202341
      type: {class: StringSettingValue, ns: , asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: By selecting Tools->LoadSystem->Export Group Values you will open
          the export window. Select from the Assets folder any type of Group Values.
          Then select the file format, click 'Export' and save it anywhere.
    - rid: 7221127563055202343
      type: {class: StringSettingValue, ns: , asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: 'LoaderMono solves that and implements ALoader to save the day(and
          your data). This component allows you to set, get, load and save data in
          runtime. Yippie!

          It works the same as Group Values as it provides
          an abstraction layer. As mentioned before you can set your GroupValues
          name and click on AutoFindPathByName to have everything set up. '
    - rid: 7221127563055202345
      type: {class: StringSettingValue, ns: , asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: 'It is up to the user to decide when to load or save data.

          In
          project settings you can set the encryption method and change the password(For
          more information read Encryption or ProjectSettings). '
    - rid: 7221127563055202347
      type: {class: StringSettingValue, ns: , asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: Loaders will throw exceptions when path is not correctly set, Group
          Values is not found or when there is no Group Values to save. Be careful
          and keep these things in mind.
    - rid: 7221127563055202349
      type: {class: StringSettingValue, ns: , asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: Group Values is the core of this tool. It is the basic element that
          allows data storage. It acts as an interface to provide structure and easy
          manipulation of data. In fact you can set values or add information going
          to the SO through the inspector(but that is not the smartest way to do
          it)
    - rid: 7221127563055202350
      type: {class: StringSettingValue, ns: , asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: This tool allows an easier way to add more entries or more fields,
          make changes to entry values and much more. It is the best way to manage
          your Group Values as when applying changes it will also save it to json.
    - rid: 7221127563055202353
      type: {class: StringSettingValue, ns: , asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: Go to Tools->LoadSystem->Group Values Editor. Do not be overwhelmed
          by the ammount of buttons we will cover them one by one.
    - rid: 7221127563055202355
      type: {class: StringSettingValue, ns: , asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: The first thing you will see is that GroupValues is in color blue.
          This is a toolbar that tells you that you are currently working on Group
          Values. You can change it to edit Templates too.
    - rid: 7221127563055202357
      type: {class: StringSettingValue, ns: , asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: This button is pretty much self-explanatory. It opens the LoadSystem
          project settings.
    - rid: 7221127563055202359
      type: {class: StringSettingValue, ns: , asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: Just in case your freshly created GV(Group Values) does not show on
          the list below you can click on this button to make sure it appears.
    - rid: 7221127563055202361
      type: {class: StringSettingValue, ns: , asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: Inside the list it provides it sets the GV that you will work on.
    - rid: 7221127581130555393
      type: {class: StringSettingValue, ns: , asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: All buttons stated appear for both workplaces. However similarities
          end here and each class posseses its own buttons with equivalent behaviour.
    - rid: 7221127581130555395
      type: {class: StringSettingValue, ns: , asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: It creates a new GV SO wherever you want.
    - rid: 7221127581130555397
      type: {class: StringSettingValue, ns: , asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: Sets all GV values to default. It does not keep in mind templates.
    - rid: 7221127581130555399
      type: {class: StringSettingValue, ns: , asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: Copies the current working GV and lets you save it wherever you want
    - rid: 7221127581130555401
      type: {class: StringSettingValue, ns: , asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: This is the most important button as it saves all changes and creates
          the json file.
